1
00:00:00,580 --> 00:00:01,775
On one coding test,

2
00:00:01,785 --> 00:00:05,138
OpenAI's cheaper model came within half a point of

3
00:00:05,148 --> 00:00:06,299
its flagship.

4
00:00:06,899 --> 00:00:09,779
And it cost about a fifth as much on that test.

5
00:00:10,129 --> 00:00:12,330
So when would you actually need Astra?

6
00:00:14,060 --> 00:00:15,643
On November 17th,

7
00:00:15,653 --> 00:00:21,220
OpenAI released two models under one name: GPT-6.2.

8
00:00:21,570 --> 00:00:23,199
Astra is the flagship.

9
00:00:23,349 --> 00:00:26,587
OpenAI pitches it at the hardest projects,

10
00:00:26,597 --> 00:00:29,547
the long, messy work where one wrong assumption can

11
00:00:29,557 --> 00:00:31,463
cost you an afternoon.

12
00:00:31,813 --> 00:00:33,164
Sol is the efficient one,

13
00:00:33,174 --> 00:00:35,120
built for everyday work.

14
00:00:35,270 --> 00:00:36,965
And the price gap is real.

15
00:00:37,415 --> 00:00:38,687
In the API,

16
00:00:38,697 --> 00:00:42,034
Astra costs $8 per million input tokens,

17
00:00:42,044 --> 00:00:44,855
and $40 per million output.

18
00:00:45,155 --> 00:00:47,119
Sol is $2 and $10.

19
00:00:47,419 --> 00:00:48,798
That's a quarter of the price,

20
00:00:48,808 --> 00:00:50,213
token for token.

21
00:00:50,663 --> 00:00:52,691
So here's why each one matters.

22
00:00:52,841 --> 00:00:54,834
If you run lots of routine jobs,

23
00:00:54,844 --> 00:00:57,925
Sol makes your budget go four times further.

24
00:00:58,075 --> 00:01:00,622
If you've got one job that has to be right,

25
00:01:00,632 --> 00:01:02,893
Astra is the one built to carry it.

26
00:01:04,423 --> 00:01:06,603
Now, the result that caught my eye.

27
00:01:06,753 --> 00:01:11,379
FrontierCode 2.0 is a coding benchmark run by Cognition.

28
00:01:11,529 --> 00:01:13,763
It doesn't just check whether the code runs.

29
00:01:13,913 --> 00:01:16,291
It asks whether a project's maintainers would

30
00:01:16,301 --> 00:01:18,264
actually merge the change.

31
00:01:18,414 --> 00:01:19,346
That's a high bar,

32
00:01:19,356 --> 00:01:21,685
and it looks a lot like real work.

33
00:01:21,835 --> 00:01:24,423
Both models ran at max reasoning effort.

34
00:01:25,223 --> 00:01:27,514
Astra scored 44.2.

35
00:01:28,114 --> 00:01:30,305
Sol scored 43.8.

36
00:01:30,755 --> 00:01:33,134
That's a gap of 0.4 points.

37
00:01:34,084 --> 00:01:35,429
Now look at the bill.

38
00:01:35,929 --> 00:01:37,093
On this benchmark,

39
00:01:37,103 --> 00:01:40,818
Astra cost about $5.97 per task.

40
00:01:41,368 --> 00:01:43,737
Sol cost about $1.29.

41
00:01:44,187 --> 00:01:45,489
Roughly a fifth.

42
00:01:46,439 --> 00:01:48,647
Here's how OpenAI sums it up.

43
00:01:48,797 --> 00:01:51,409
Sol comes within half a point of Astra,

44
00:01:51,419 --> 00:01:53,109
at about one-fifth the cost,

45
00:01:53,119 --> 00:01:54,643
with both at max.

46
00:01:55,193 --> 00:01:56,411
Two caveats.

47
00:01:56,561 --> 00:01:58,858
These are OpenAI-reported numbers,

48
00:01:58,868 --> 00:02:00,506
from one benchmark.

49
00:02:00,656 --> 00:02:02,699
And that cost is per task on this test,

50
00:02:02,709 --> 00:02:04,970
not a quote for your workload.

51
00:02:05,520 --> 00:02:07,530
But it does tell you something useful.

52
00:02:07,680 --> 00:02:09,748
On well-defined coding changes,

53
00:02:09,758 --> 00:02:12,161
the flagship isn't buying you much.

54
00:02:12,311 --> 00:02:13,978
So where does the gap show up?

55
00:02:15,608 --> 00:02:19,255
Scene one. A small bakery needs a landing page.

56
00:02:19,405 --> 00:02:20,666
Hero image, menu,

57
00:02:20,676 --> 00:02:23,100
opening hours, a contact form.

58
00:02:23,250 --> 00:02:24,764
You've got a wireframe,

59
00:02:24,774 --> 00:02:27,951
and that's exactly the kind of job FrontierCode

60
00:02:27,961 --> 00:02:29,475
rewards: a clear spec,

61
00:02:29,485 --> 00:02:30,444
a few files,

62
00:02:30,454 --> 00:02:33,061
and an easy way to check the result.

63
00:02:33,411 --> 00:02:34,974
Then the complication.

64
00:02:35,124 --> 00:02:37,320
The owner wants it to work on phones,

65
00:02:37,330 --> 00:02:41,513
load fast, and send form messages straight to their inbox.

66
00:02:41,663 --> 00:02:42,994
And after the first draft,

67
00:02:43,004 --> 00:02:45,468
they'll want three rounds of changes.

68
00:02:45,618 --> 00:02:46,447
That's more work,

69
00:02:46,457 --> 00:02:48,526
but it's still well-defined work.

70
00:02:48,676 --> 00:02:49,665
If the layout breaks,

71
00:02:49,675 --> 00:02:50,559
you see it.

72
00:02:50,569 --> 00:02:51,549
If the form fails,

73
00:02:51,559 --> 00:02:53,125
you know in seconds.

74
00:02:53,575 --> 00:02:54,985
My pick here is Sol.

75
00:02:55,335 --> 00:02:58,090
When a mistake is cheap to spot and cheap to fix,

76
00:02:58,100 --> 00:02:59,901
you don't need the flagship.

77
00:03:00,051 --> 00:03:02,233
At that benchmark's cost ratio,

78
00:03:02,243 --> 00:03:05,315
you could run about five Sol passes for the price

79
00:03:05,325 --> 00:03:06,879
of one Astra pass.

80
00:03:08,509 --> 00:03:10,616
Scene two is a different animal.

81
00:03:10,766 --> 00:03:12,819
Checkout totals are off by a cent,

82
00:03:12,829 --> 00:03:14,432
but only sometimes.

83
00:03:14,582 --> 00:03:16,255
The error shows up on the receipt,

84
00:03:16,265 --> 00:03:18,079
but the cause isn't there.

85
00:03:18,229 --> 00:03:19,755
It runs back through the cart,

86
00:03:19,765 --> 00:03:20,907
a currency helper,

87
00:03:20,917 --> 00:03:24,380
and a discount rule someone changed months ago.

88
00:03:24,530 --> 00:03:27,149
You can only reproduce it with the right coupon,

89
00:03:27,159 --> 00:03:28,772
in the right currency.

90
00:03:29,122 --> 00:03:30,536
Here's the complication:

91
00:03:30,546 --> 00:03:32,942
every file looks fine on its own.

92
00:03:33,092 --> 00:03:35,045
The bug is in how they connect.

93
00:03:35,195 --> 00:03:37,387
And a wrong fix doesn't fail loudly.

94
00:03:37,397 --> 00:03:40,179
It ships, and quietly charges the wrong amount.

95
00:03:40,629 --> 00:03:42,742
This is where I'd reach for Astra.

96
00:03:43,092 --> 00:03:45,096
Not because Sol can't read code,

97
00:03:45,106 --> 00:03:47,954
but because a confident wrong answer costs more

98
00:03:47,964 --> 00:03:49,654
than the price difference.

99
00:03:49,804 --> 00:03:52,677
Let Sol reproduce the bug and add logging.

100
00:03:52,687 --> 00:03:54,496
Then hand the fix to Astra.

101
00:03:56,126 --> 00:03:58,613
Scene three. You've got rough meeting notes,

102
00:03:58,623 --> 00:04:01,431
and you need a ten-slide deck by tomorrow.

103
00:04:01,581 --> 00:04:03,044
Fragments, a few numbers,

104
00:04:03,054 --> 00:04:05,035
half an idea for a chart.

105
00:04:05,385 --> 00:04:06,641
For an internal update,

106
00:04:06,651 --> 00:04:07,788
Sol is fine.

107
00:04:07,938 --> 00:04:09,988
It'll turn the notes into an outline,

108
00:04:09,998 --> 00:04:11,548
headlines and speaker notes,

109
00:04:11,558 --> 00:04:14,382
and you'll tidy the rest in a few minutes.

110
00:04:14,832 --> 00:04:16,347
Now change the job.

111
00:04:16,697 --> 00:04:19,757
The deck has to follow a strict brand template:

112
00:04:19,767 --> 00:04:21,571
fixed fonts, locked layouts,

113
00:04:21,581 --> 00:04:24,065
a legal footer on every slide.

114
00:04:24,215 --> 00:04:26,339
And the source isn't notes anymore.

115
00:04:26,349 --> 00:04:29,007
It's a forty-page report and a spreadsheet,

116
00:04:29,017 --> 00:04:31,082
and every figure has to match.

117
00:04:31,232 --> 00:04:34,023
Now, one wrong number in front of a client is the

118
00:04:34,033 --> 00:04:35,632
expensive mistake.

119
00:04:36,082 --> 00:04:37,920
That's when I'd switch to Astra.

120
00:04:38,070 --> 00:04:39,476
Same kind of task.

121
00:04:39,486 --> 00:04:40,724
Different stakes.

122
00:04:42,454 --> 00:04:44,009
So here's the verdict.

123
00:04:44,159 --> 00:04:45,843
Under Sol: the bakery site,

124
00:04:45,853 --> 00:04:46,859
the internal deck,

125
00:04:46,869 --> 00:04:49,495
and most routine code changes.

126
00:04:49,645 --> 00:04:52,300
It came within half a point on that coding test,

127
00:04:52,310 --> 00:04:54,062
at a fraction of the cost.

128
00:04:54,412 --> 00:04:56,489
Under Astra: the cross-file money bug,

129
00:04:56,499 --> 00:04:59,434
and the client deck with a strict template.

130
00:04:59,884 --> 00:05:01,530
And the switch rule is simple.

131
00:05:01,780 --> 00:05:02,994
Start with Sol.

132
00:05:03,144 --> 00:05:06,361
Move to Astra when the work spans lots of connected

133
00:05:06,371 --> 00:05:09,049
pieces, when you can't check the result quickly,

134
00:05:09,059 --> 00:05:12,541
or when one mistake costs more than the price gap.

135
00:05:12,691 --> 00:05:13,991
If none of those are true,

136
00:05:14,001 --> 00:05:15,825
Sol is probably enough.

137
00:05:16,275 --> 00:05:17,718
Sol is your default.

138
00:05:17,728 --> 00:05:19,498
Astra is your escalation.

